A||||12||fieroc4||Coil overs||||5||
Z||000000||fieroc4||01-31-2000||01:43 AM||||Am I correct in assuming that using coil overs in front lets you get rid of the front springs, or is it dependant on the brand of coil over used?<P>Dan||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||01-31-2000||03:56 AM||||Yes, it would let you get rid of the front springs.  A coil-over shock is just what it says, the spring coil is mounted on the shock body.  By definition the rear struts on the Fiero are coil-over's, but most people assume that when you say 'coil-over' you mean a high performance, adjustable unit.  <BR>Front coil-overs are <B>expensive</B> for the Fiero, and require replacement of at least the top(?) a-arm.  i don't think you would really need them unless you're a serious auto crosser..||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||01-31-2000||04:29 AM||||But if you get rear "coil overs" for the rear, aren't they a smaller diameter allowing for a slightly wider rim/larger backset?<P>fieroc4--If your interest in coil overs for the front has anything to do with lowering your Fiero, try dropped spindles instead.  About half the cost.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||02-01-2000||01:38 AM||||Yes, aftermarket coil-overs use smaller diameter springs.  I'm just saying that the Macpherson struts are by definition coil-overs.  sorry if I confused anybody..  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-01-2000||04:25 PM||||Standard--I understood what you were saying.  I just wanted to make sure that coil overs (at least what everyone thinks of when they talk about them) have a smaller diameter.  I'll be using them in the rear at some point.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||02-02-2000||01:57 AM||||Geez, get me all worried over nothing!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I plan on putting coil-overs in the rear of my next fiero, too.  Prices for rear coil-overs seem to be pretty good.||||reg||6
Z||000006||Phaeton||02-02-2000||02:24 AM||||I'm confused.  I read on another thread that if the springs are blue and the shocks are red then the Fiero goes faster.  Now I am to understand that painting red and blue coils over both causes the Fiero to go racing fast?  Can I get a picture?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||02-02-2000||07:01 AM||||That's true only if the calipers are painted also.Something about balance in the wind resistance or the like.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||02-02-2000||06:26 PM||||The calipers MUST be yellow for this to work.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spyder||02-02-2000||07:41 PM||||CLIFF... the other kids are picking on me!<BR>I only painted the calipers yellow.  The Suspension Techniques springs come from the factory blue and the Koni shocks and struts come from the factory red.  <BR>Before someone believes that the colors add horsepower, we are joking. (you know someone would believe)<BR>What the color combination really does: It makes the car stop quicker and adds .09 Gs on the skid pad.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000010||Monkeyman||02-02-2000||08:48 PM||||Who's joking?!||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||02-03-2000||10:35 AM||||It should be noted that the Caliper, Spring, Shock color modifications only work correctly if you have a <B>NEUSPEED</B> logo placed diagonally on your hood, or on each side of the car.<P>You can increase your gas mileage as well if you get a sticker from each vendor you buy the mods from and put them on your back windshield.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||02-03-2000||05:09 PM||||Unless your wheels/tires stick out more than 2 1/2 feet from your fenderwells.  In that case you would lose fuel mileage but gain anywhere from 60-114.3 hp.||||reg||1
